# /dev/uinput defaults to root:root mode 0660 -- without this rule, only
# root could create a virtual gamepad via inputtino (keyboard/mouse/touch
# stay on KWin's own fake_input mechanism regardless -- see design.md/the
# virtual-input-devices plan for why). Grants the "input" group access
# instead, matching the reference project Wolf's own 85-wolf.rules line for
# the same purpose. redfog-server (see redfog-server.service's
# SupplementaryGroups=input) is the only thing that needs this; harmless
# no-op if REDFOG_INPUT_BACKEND is never set to "inputtino" (the default,
# "fake-input", never touches /dev/uinput).
#
# TAG+="uaccess" kept deliberately (not stripped), even though other
# software already tags this same shared device (Steam's/KDE Connect's own
# udev rules, both `KERNEL=="uinput" ... TAG+="uaccess"` with no GROUP= of
# their own) -- matching Moonshine's own 60-moonshine.rules, which combines
# GROUP=/MODE= with TAG+="uaccess" the same way. Confirmed live: systemd's
# per-session ACL sync (`devnode_acl()`) only ever manages named-*user* ACL
# entries, never the ACL's own `group::` entry -- so once genuinely
# correct, this group grant is stable across every future login/logout/VT
# switch, and coexists peacefully with Steam/KDE Connect's own *interactive*
# per-session access to the same device (no need to choose one or the
# other). Getting `group::` genuinely correct the first time on a device
# that already carries a *stale* ACL from before this rule existed needs an
# explicit one-time `setfacl -b` (see redfog-git.install) -- a plain
# reload/trigger, or even a full module unload+reload, does not reset an
# already-stale ACL's `group::` entry on its own; confirmed live.
KERNEL=="uinput", SUBSYSTEM=="misc", MODE="0660", GROUP="input", OPTIONS+="static_node=uinput", TAG+="uaccess"

# The rule above only covers /dev/uinput itself (needed to *create* a
# device); each created gamepad's own /dev/input/eventN + /dev/input/jsN
# nodes are a *different* udev device (SUBSYSTEM=="input") that the
# distro's own stock uaccess rules already fall back to GROUP="input"
# MODE="0660" for. That fallback is a blanket grant -- any local user who
# happens to be a member of "input" (regardless of whether they're
# streaming a redfog session at all, let alone *this* one) could open
# another session's virtual gamepad directly from the real host /dev,
# something redfog-broker's own per-session BindPaths sandboxing
# (device_sandbox_systemd_directives) can't do anything about, since that
# grant lives entirely outside any session's mount namespace. Also
# deliberately *not* relying on the "uaccess" mechanism at all here
# (unlike /dev/uinput above), and not just because it grants to whichever
# user is the *active* session on this device's arbitrary, unrelated seat
# rather than this device's own session's target user. Confirmed live
# (2026-09-21) it's actively hostile to anything else touching this
# device's ACL, not just a loose fit: 70-uaccess.rules unconditionally
# tags every SUBSYSTEM=="input" device with ENV{ID_INPUT_JOYSTICK}=="?*"
# (unavoidably true for us) as "uaccess", and 73-seat-late.rules's
# `RUN{builtin}+="uaccess"` (gated on that tag) then keeps *re-writing*
# the device's ACL down to just the active-seat-session's user on every
# subsequent uevent -- observed clobbering redfog-broker's own grant
# within the same second. Tried and abandoned: opting out via
# `TAG-="uaccess"` from this (later-loaded) file -- doesn't work, udev's
# tag *match* conditions (like 73-seat-late.rules') check the sticky/
# historical tag set, which a later `-=` never clears, by design. Also
# tried and abandoned: a separate, earlier-loaded file doing
# `GOTO="uaccess_end"` to skip 70-uaccess.rules' body entirely for these
# devices -- `udevadm test` confirmed this is a structural dead end, not
# a syntax mistake: GOTO targets resolve in file-*loading* order, so an
# earlier file can never jump forward into a label a later file defines
# ("no matching label, ignoring" / "dropping"). The actual, robust fix:
# stop using an ACL at all. `devnode_acl()`/the "uaccess" builtin only
# ever manage *named ACL entries* -- never base ownership, confirmed
# already true of the `group::` entry above and equally true of plain
# `chown`. redfog-broker's own explicit per-session `chown <username>`
# (session.rs's `grant_acl` — actually a chown for these nodes, not a
# setfacl; matched by the distinctive name `InputtinoGamepad::new` gives
# every device: "redfog Gamepad (<username>-<session_id>)") is the sole
# real access grant from here on, immune to uaccess entirely since
# there's no ACL for it to resync.
#
# ENV{ID_SEAT}="" (rather than leaving it unset, the default for basically
# every device on a single-seat machine): libinput's own device_added()
# (src/udev-seat.c) only falls back to its "seat0" default when this
# property is *absent* -- `if (!device_seat) device_seat = default_seat;`
# -- an *explicit* empty value skips that fallback and is compared as-is.
# KWin's headless `--virtual` backend has no real logind seat, so it calls
# `libinput_udev_assign_seat()` with an empty string too (confirmed via
# its own source, see the plan/TODO's libei note) -- meaning libinput's
# `strcmp(device_seat, input->seat_id)` device-matching can otherwise
# never succeed for *any* uinput device under it, gamepad included. This
# doesn't matter for redfog's own gamepad handling today (it reads the
# node directly, bypassing libinput entirely -- see
# `InputtinoGamepad`'s doc comment) but costs nothing and may help any
# *other* libinput-based app/toolkit running inside the same headless
# session that expects normal seat-based device discovery to work.
#
# Not TAG+="uaccess" (unlike /dev/uinput above): confirmed live
# (2026-09-21) that these devices must never carry that tag at all, or
# systemd-logind's own `uaccess` udev builtin (invoked by the stock
# 73-seat-late.rules, gated on the tag) keeps re-writing this device's
# ACL down to whichever session it considers "active" on the (real,
# physical, entirely unrelated) seat -- observed clobbering redfog-
# broker's own just-applied `user:<username>:rw` grant within the same
# second. Trying to *remove* the tag after the fact from here (a later
# file, via `TAG-="uaccess"`) does not work, even though that's real,
# documented udev syntax -- see 05-redfog-input-preempt-uaccess.rules
# (loaded first, before the stock rule that assigns it) for why, and for
# the actual fix. redfog-broker's own explicit per-session `setfacl`
# (session.rs's grant_acl) is the sole thing that ever touches this ACL.
SUBSYSTEM=="input", ATTRS{name}=="redfog Gamepad *", GROUP="root", MODE="0600", ENV{ID_SEAT}=""
